00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:57,940
We now return to High Explosives on Modern Marvels.

125
00:10:01,780 --> 00:10:06,000
While gunpowder had powered the military machines of the world for centuries,

126
00:10:06,420 --> 00:10:10,760
a new and more powerful explosive was finding a home on the world's battlefields.

127
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:16,520
Based on coal oil, TNT was part of a new breed of chemical, the high explosive.

128
00:10:17,300 --> 00:10:19,680
High explosives burn at hypersonic speeds.

129
00:10:20,160 --> 00:10:25,100
The gases released then form deadly shock waves that shear and shatter anything in their path.

130
00:10:25,820 --> 00:10:30,600
This enhanced destructiveness soon migrated from the battlefields of Europe to the sea.

131
00:10:31,420 --> 00:10:35,420
The First World War found floating battle wagons of unprecedented size.

132
00:10:35,500 --> 00:10:39,520
On these enormous ships were guns larger than ever seen at sea.

133
00:10:39,521 --> 00:10:48,540
The shells fired by the big 14-inch naval guns were huge, weighing nearly a ton.

134
00:10:49,080 --> 00:10:52,140
They could be fired up to nine miles with ravaging effects.

135
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:55,960
Large crews were needed to handle the enormous explosives.

136
00:10:57,420 --> 00:11:02,240
Projectiles arrived inverted on the upper hoist in a shell car.

137
00:11:02,740 --> 00:11:06,880
When they reached the top, they were tilted backwards onto a transfer tray

138
00:11:06,881 --> 00:11:09,040
and rolled onto the loading tray.

139
00:11:09,520 --> 00:11:12,060
where they were then rammed into the weapon.

140
00:11:12,061 --> 00:11:14,240
Once the projectile had been seated,

141
00:11:14,241 --> 00:11:17,440
powder bags were passed up from the gun pit below

142
00:11:17,441 --> 00:11:19,040
in groups of two.

143
00:11:19,041 --> 00:11:20,920
They were placed on the rammer tray

144
00:11:20,921 --> 00:11:24,660
and rammed in by a team of two men using a wooden pole.

145
00:11:24,661 --> 00:11:27,800
Once all four bags, or 420 pounds of powder,

146
00:11:27,801 --> 00:11:31,040
had been loaded, the breach was swung closed

147
00:11:31,041 --> 00:11:34,480
and a cartridge was placed in the firing mechanism

148
00:11:34,481 --> 00:11:36,460
for the subsequent round.

149
00:11:36,461 --> 00:11:39,180
About 16 men operated this level of the turret.

150
00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:43,560
Temperatures in here could raise to approximately 120 degrees.

151
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:51,080
While naval artillery claimed victories on the surface,

152
00:11:51,500 --> 00:11:55,400
the balance of power was shifting to explosives fired from beneath the waves.

153
00:11:57,040 --> 00:12:01,200
Most of the submarine technology in the First World War was very low technology.

154
00:12:01,760 --> 00:12:04,860
It was really a matter of putting explosives into a tin fish

155
00:12:04,861 --> 00:12:09,280
and sending it out propelled by compressed air with a contact detonator,

156
00:12:09,640 --> 00:12:11,540
which exploded when it contacted something.

157
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:16,520
This silent killer packing up to 500 pounds of TNT

158
00:12:16,521 --> 00:12:20,120
was a new twist in the stealthy delivery of high explosives

159
00:12:20,121 --> 00:12:22,920
and shook the navies of the world to their core.

160
00:12:23,680 --> 00:12:25,820
From nearly three quarters of a mile away,

161
00:12:25,821 --> 00:12:28,960
a submarine could sink the largest ship with impunity.

162
00:12:30,800 --> 00:12:33,460
Back on land, it was not large explosives,

163
00:12:33,461 --> 00:12:37,160
but the rapid-fire machine gun that was revolutionizing warfare.

164
00:12:38,620 --> 00:12:41,740
The First World War was really dominated by the machine gun and the trench

165
00:12:41,741 --> 00:12:43,160
and very high casualties.

166
00:12:44,100 --> 00:12:49,340
A two- or three-man team could put a large amount of firepower down very quickly.

167
00:12:49,900 --> 00:12:53,080
If you had lots of machine guns, the infantry could not really move

168
00:12:53,081 --> 00:12:54,840
because there was so much firepower there.

169
00:12:57,400 --> 00:13:02,060
The advent of stable explosive powders allowed for the development of rapid-fire weapons.

170
00:13:02,600 --> 00:13:05,620
Hundreds of bullets could be fired in just minutes from a single gun.

171
00:13:06,640 --> 00:13:09,800
Particularly deadly was the British Vickers heavy machine gun,

172
00:13:10,280 --> 00:13:13,960
capable of a sustained rate of fire of 10,000 rounds per hour.

173
00:13:14,660 --> 00:13:20,280
Produced from 1912 to 1966, it was one of the most enduring designs of the century.

174
00:13:21,480 --> 00:13:23,840
Machine guns brought the trench war to a standstill.

175
00:13:24,040 --> 00:13:28,420
It would take one more technological advance to break the stalemate of the Great War.

176
00:13:28,421 --> 00:13:34,500
The first tank as we know it really evolved during the First World War.

177
00:13:34,501 --> 00:13:40,100
In the early days of the war, the Allied forces did use armoured cars, but as the static situation

178
00:13:40,101 --> 00:13:44,660
developed, trenches, barbed wire, obviously armoured cars could not cross this type of

179
00:13:44,661 --> 00:13:45,660
terrain.

180
00:13:45,661 --> 00:13:51,160
So tanks were developed in order to cross a terrain swept by machine gun fire, covered

181
00:13:51,161 --> 00:13:58,400
